--TEST--
`Core\Reflect::steps` reads a path converted from text at run time the same way as a written one, and a call through a callable value throws a `LogicError`, because it knows no class to start at — `rule:core-classes/str-parts-and-reflect-steps`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A path built while the program runs needs `as propertyPath<Order>`.
// `Core\Reflect::steps` then reads it like a written path.
class Customer {
    public string $email = "";
    private string $password = "";
}

class Order {
    public ?Customer $customer = null;
}

var $text = Core\Str::join(["customer", "email"], ".");
var $path = $text as propertyPath<Order>;
foreach (Core\Reflect::steps($path) as var $step) {
    echo $step->name(), ": ", $step->type() ?? "no type", "\n";
}

// A private property is not part of any path, so the conversion fails first.
try {
    var $hidden = Core\Str::join(["customer", "password"], ".") as propertyPath<Order>;
    echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Reflect::steps($hidden)), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $failed) {
    echo "conversion failed\n";
}

// A callable value does not know which class the path starts at.
callable $steps = Core\Reflect::steps;
try {
    $steps($path);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
customer: ?Customer
email: string
conversion failed
Core\Reflect::steps was called through a callable value, so the class the path starts at is not known
